  {
    "waitForText": {
      "id": "lf_inline_modal_title",
      "value": "Pay Yourself First"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "xpath=//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/p",
      "value": "Tired of living paycheck to paycheck? One proven way to help you reach financial freedom is to get into the habit of paying yourself first. This doesn't mean spending on yourself. Instead, it involves saving before doing anything else. You set aside a portion of your paycheck before you pay your other bills. This helps ensure that your non-immediate financial goals are covered, such as building up an emergency fund, saving for a down payment on a home, contributing to a college fund, etc."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "xpath=//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/p[2]",
      "value": "Good habits can take a long time to develop because the benefit is usually not immediate. One easy way to get into the habit of paying yourself first is to make it automatic. Split your direct deposit so that an amount or a percentage goes directly into your savings account before you can spend it. Or, set up an automatic transfer for each payday, regularly sending money from your checking account to your savings account. How much you choose to save consistently will depend on your budget and goals."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "xpath=//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/p[3]",
      "value": "Once you have set up an automated savings plan, don't touch it unless absolutely necessary. Treat the money you've saved as if it's off-limits, except for its intended purpose or a true financial emergency. If you don't think that you're saving enough for your goal, look to boost your income or find costs that you can reduce or eliminate such as eating out less often."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "xpath=//button[@id='lf_modal_close']/span",
      "value": "X"
    }
  },
  {
    "click": {
      "id": "lf_modal_close"
    }
  },
  {
    "click": {
      "id": "lf_glossary:CreateBudget"
    }
  },
  {
    "waitForText": {
      "id": "lf_inline_modal_title",
      "value": "Understanding Liquidity"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "xpath=//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/p",
      "value": "Not all savings are equal. In order to control your ability to access your money when you need it, you need to pay attention to how \"liquid\" the money will be in each type of account. Each type of account has its own rules for withdrawing money. For example, a regular savings account allows you to withdraw any or all of your money at any time, without penalty, generally on the day of your request. A CD, however, has restrictions that make your money not as \"liquid.\" A 3-month CD, for example, means your money can't be withdrawn until three months after you open the CD account, unless you're willing to pay an early withdrawal penalty."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "xpath=//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/p[2]",
      "value": "Match liquidity with need. When building an emergency fund, be sure your money is in the most liquid type of account, one that lets you get your money quickly, especially in case of emergencies. These accounts will generally offer the lowest rates of interest, but that's the trade-off for the liquidity. Money being saved for months and years later can be in accounts with less liquidity, and typically higher potential returns."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "xpath=//button[@id='lf_modal_close']/span",
      "value": "X"
    }
  },
